1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,519
How big should your emergency fund be?

2
00:00:02,939 --> 00:00:04,920
Not the internet's number. Yours.

3
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:09,911
First, what it's for. Losing your job. A medical bill. A car that won't start.

4
00:00:10,331 --> 00:00:17,682
Not the holidays, or car registration. Those are on the calendar, so they belong in sinking funds. That's episode three.

5
00:00:18,452 --> 00:00:20,547
So let's find your number, in three steps.

6
00:00:21,317 --> 00:00:23,614
Step one. Count essentials, not income.

7
00:00:24,034 --> 00:00:29,416
If your income stopped tomorrow, what would you still have to pay? Here's our example from episode one.

8
00:00:29,836 --> 00:00:36,188
That's $3,011 a month. Fun money, streaming and eating out don't count. You'd cut those first.

9
00:00:36,958 --> 00:00:38,668
Step two. Pick how many months.

10
00:00:39,088 --> 00:00:41,946
The usual range is three to six months of essentials.

11
00:00:42,366 --> 00:00:49,185
Lean toward six if your income is irregular, you're the only earner, you have kids, or your job would be hard to replace.

12
00:00:49,605 --> 00:00:55,617
For our example, 3 months is $9,033. Six months is about $18,000.

13
00:00:56,387 --> 00:01:00,064
Step three. Build it in stages, so it never feels impossible.

14
00:01:00,484 --> 00:01:09,751
Start with a starter goal: one month of bills, $2,371. At $453 a month (the money freed up once the credit card is gone), that's 6 months.

15
00:01:10,171 --> 00:01:12,681
Three full months at that pace takes twenty months.

16
00:01:13,101 --> 00:01:16,883
But send your two three-paycheck-month checks there too, and it's about a year.

17
00:01:17,303 --> 00:01:27,344
Keep it somewhere boring. A separate high-yield savings account, ideally at a different bank. Easy to reach in an emergency, but not so easy you spend it on a Tuesday.

18
00:01:27,764 --> 00:01:32,934
And if you use it, that's what it's for. Pause the extras, refill it, then get back to your goals.

19
00:01:33,704 --> 00:01:38,475
The Paycheck Planner in the description keeps your bills, funds and goals in one place.

20
00:01:38,895 --> 00:01:41,650
Subscribe for more money math, with real numbers.
